# Differential test of tuple-arrays-equality.js: strict equality of a row's
# Number|String value against '', null and the global `undefined`, then the
# kept rows joined. The C++ is compiled against public Core, checked for
# interpreter symbols with nm, run, and every global compared with the
# interpreter's (compilation-unit.py). The negative prefixes the printed
# String `line`, by name, and requires the comparison to fail naming it.
# compile-clean.py checks a warning-free build on both toolchains.

# RUN: python3 %S/tuple-arrays-equality.py --translate ctjs-translate --opt ctjs-opt --js %S/tuple-arrays-equality.js --out %t/tuple-arrays-equality.emitc.mlir
# RUN: %compilation_unit --module %t/tuple-arrays-equality.emitc.mlir --js %S/tuple-arrays-equality.js --work %t --name tuple_arrays_equality --core-build %build
# RUN: not %compilation_unit --module %t/tuple-arrays-equality.emitc.mlir --js %S/tuple-arrays-equality.js --work %t --name tuple_arrays_equality_negative --core-build %build --mutate line --mutate-as string 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NEGATIVE
# RUN: %compile_clean --module %t/tuple-arrays-equality.emitc.mlir --work %t --name tuple_arrays_equality

# NEGATIVE: global 'line' differs
